# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release archives (`git archive`, and GitHub's generated source zips/tarballs)
#
# An Omeka S theme is installed by unzipping into `themes/`, so the archive is
# the product: everything left in it lands on a production server. Keep it to
# what Omeka actually loads — templates, helpers, compiled assets, compiled
# translations, config, licence — plus the documentation and the published
# token contract that describe the thing being installed, and strip the whole
# development toolchain.
#
# "Docs" was too coarse a rule to settle three files by, so the line is: the
# archive ships documentation ABOUT the theme (docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md,
# DESIGN-PHILOSOPHY.md, MIRADOR.md, PWA.md, README.md, and tokens.json, whose
# prose half ships beside it) and does NOT ship the artifact layer that TOOLING
# reads (DESIGN.md, PRODUCT.md, .impeccable/) or point-in-time planning
# (ROADMAP.md, docs/IMPECCABLE-ROADMAP.md, docs/DESIGN-REVIEW-2026-06.md).
#
# The release workflow enforces both halves of that sentence, and does not
# restate this list: it derives the exclusions from the rules below, and
# separately allowlists the archive's root, because a denylist can only catch a
# rule that stopped matching — never a file nobody thought to add a rule for.
# That omission is exactly how DESIGN.md and PRODUCT.md shipped for ten
# releases. Verify after any change with:
#
#     git archive --prefix=IWAC-theme/ -o t.zip HEAD && unzip -l t.zip
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Git and CI plumbing
/.gitattributes   export-ignore
/.gitignore       export-ignore
/.github/         export-ignore
/.editorconfig    export-ignore

# Node build toolchain — the theme ships compiled CSS, never builds on the server
/package.json         export-ignore
/package-lock.json    export-ignore
/gulpfile.js          export-ignore
/.nvmrc               export-ignore
/scripts/             export-ignore

# Tests and fixtures
/test/                export-ignore
/test-support/        export-ignore
/e2e/                 export-ignore
/playwright.config.js export-ignore

# Agent and contributor working notes
/CLAUDE.md      export-ignore
/.impeccable.md export-ignore
/.impeccable/   export-ignore
/.agents/       export-ignore
/.claude/       export-ignore
/ROADMAP.md     export-ignore

# The rest of the Impeccable artifact layer. These are read by tooling, not by
# a reader of the theme: DESIGN.md is generated by `/impeccable document` and
# machine-checked against tokens.json, and PRODUCT.md carries a
# `impeccable:product-schema` marker. They joined /.impeccable/ above after
# shipping to the live server in every release from 2.10.0 to 2.20.0 — the
# directory was export-ignored in 2.15 and these two, which sit at the root,
# were not.
/DESIGN.md      export-ignore
/PRODUCT.md     export-ignore

# Point-in-time internal review, superseded by docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md
/docs/DESIGN-REVIEW-2026-06.md export-ignore

# Process, not product: how the design skill is driven against this repo, with
# status tracked in a GitHub issue. Same category as /ROADMAP.md above.
/docs/IMPECCABLE-ROADMAP.md export-ignore

# Gettext sources — only the compiled .mo is loaded at runtime
/language/*.pot export-ignore
/language/*.po  export-ignore

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated artifacts — collapse in diffs/PR review
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/asset/css/style.css linguist-generated
/tokens.json         linguist-generated
/language/fr.mo      linguist-generated

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings and binary safety
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Keep gettext source catalogs deterministic across platforms.
/language/*.po  text eol=lf
/language/*.pot text eol=lf

# Same for everything the build writes. These are the files the Quality
# workflow asserts with `git diff --exit-code`, and their writers emit LF on
# every platform. Without this a Windows checkout gets CRLF (core.autocrlf),
# the next `npm run build` rewrites them as LF, and `git status` reports three
# modified files whose content is byte-identical. Harmless in CI, which only
# ever runs on Linux — but it trains you to ignore a dirty generated file,
# which is exactly the signal that step exists to give you.
/asset/css/style.css   text eol=lf
/tokens.json           text eol=lf
/docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md text eol=lf

# Never newline-translate binaries, whatever core.autocrlf is set to locally.
*.mo   binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
